{:answers {"extract-andy-ephemeral" {"opencode-go/deepseek-v4-flash" "The note runs on six established security/ops concepts plus one author coinage in the title.\n\n1. **Sandboxing** — canonical (isolation technique in OS/security). Invoking phrase: “runs in a sandbox prepared specifically for it.”\n\n2. **Least privilege** — canonical (Saltzer & Schroeder design principle). Invoking phrase: “least privilege is the first thing you have to build.”\n\n3. **Physical access ⇒ total compromise** — canonical security axiom (no software boundary survives physical possession). Invoking phrase: “An agent with physical access to secrets will get at them sooner or later.”\n\n4. **Reproducible environments/builds** — canonical (reproducible-builds practice). Invoking phrase: “Environments are reproducible…”\n\n5. **Immutable infrastructure** — canonical (immutable-server ops pattern); the “for the duration of a tick” scoping is the author’s adaptation, not part of the canonical concept. Invoking phrase: “…and immutable for the duration of a tick.”\n\n6. **Capability-based security / no ambient authority (delegation-only rights)** — canonical (capability theory: a subject cannot grant itself authority; only a delegated authority can amplify rights). Invoking phrase: “an agent can request more rights but cannot grant them to itself.”\n\n7. **“Ephemeral agents”** (title) — the author’s own coinage/framing, naming the combination above; not an established concept.\n\nAlso present but offering no distinct theory: “its whole state is the sandbox plus a working directory” merely restates minimal-state design; “tick” is the author’s terminology for a lifecycle period.\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-pro" "| Concept | Status | Phrase that invokes it |\n|---|---|---|\n| Sandboxing / runtime isolation | CANONICAL | “Each agent runs in a sandbox prepared specifically for it.” |\n| Principle of least privilege | CANONICAL | “least privilege is the first thing you have to build.” |\n| Assume breach / eventual compromise under physical access | CANONICAL security tenet (not a formal theorem) | “An agent with physical access to secrets will get at them sooner or later.” |\n| Reproducible environments / reproducible builds | CANONICAL | “Environments are reproducible …” |\n| Immutable environments / immutable infrastructure | CANONICAL, but here scoped per lifecycle (“for the duration of a tick”) | “… immutable for the duration of a tick.” |\n| Execution tick / time-slice as a unit of agent lifetime | AUTHOR'S OWN framing; nearest canonical analogue is a scheduling quantum or lease | “… for the duration of a tick.” |\n| Request/grant authority split — no self-elevation | AUTHOR'S design framing; the backing idea is canonical authorization/least privilege | “an agent can request more rights but cannot grant them to itself.” |\n| Complete explicit state model | AUTHOR'S OWN framing; touches canonical stateless/minimal-state process design | “its whole state is the sandbox plus a working directory.” |\n| Ephemeral agents | AUTHOR'S OWN coinage/framing; nearest canonical analogue is ephemeral/short-lived compute | title, “Ephemeral agents” |\n\nThe only explicit canonical security principles in the text are **sandboxing** and **least privilege**. Reproducibility, immutability, and assume-breach are established practices/tenets; “tick,” “ephemeral agents,” the request/grant split, and the sandbox-plus-working-directory state model are Andy Smith's framing.\n", "opencode-go/glm-5.2" "Concepts invoked in the NOTE:\n\n| # | Concept | Status | Invoking phrase |\n|---|---------|--------|-----------------|\n| 1 | **Sandboxing** (isolated execution environment) | Canonical security/engineering concept | \"runs in a sandbox prepared specifically for it\" |\n| 2 | **Principle of least privilege** (Saltzer & Schroeder, 1975) | Canonical | \"least privilege is the first thing you have to build\" |\n| 3 | **\"Physical access ⇒ compromise\"** — the security folk-adage that physical/low-level access to a system eventually defeats all controls | Canonical folk wisdom in security (informal, not a formal theory) | \"An agent with physical access to secrets will get at them sooner or later\" |\n| 4 | **Reproducible environments** (reproducible builds/infra, à la Nix/Docker/Bazel) | Canonical concept | \"Environments are reproducible\" |\n| 5 | **Immutability** (immutable infrastructure / immutable state) | Canonical | \"immutable for the duration of a tick\" |\n| 6 | **Privilege-escalation prevention / separation of duty** — rights flow from an external authority, never self-granted | Canonical principle; the specific phrasing is the author's | \"can request more rights but cannot grant them to itself\" |\n| 7 | **Tick** (discrete time step in agent-based simulation) | Canonical term in simulation/agent-based modeling | \"for the duration of a tick\" |\n| 8 | **Externalized/encapsulated state** (agent as stateless process + persisted workspace, cf. stateless servers or process-vs-environment separation in OS design) | Canonical underlying idea; the \"sandbox plus working directory\" formulation is the author's framing | \"its whole state is the sandbox plus a working directory\" |\n\nAuthor's own coinages (not established concepts, per the text itself):\n\n- **\"Ephemeral agents\"** — the title/overall framing; no established body of theory is referenced by this phrase alone. It gestures at ephemerality (cf. ephemeral containers/lambda functions) but the term as used here is the author's.\n- **\"Prepared specifically for it\"** per-agent sandbox provisioning — a design decision, not an established concept name.\n\nCaveats on status: #3 is canonical as informal doctrine, not a formal principle; #7 (\"tick\") is borrowed from simulation vocabulary and its meaning here (per-tick immutability window) is the author's extension.\n", "opencode-go/kimi-k2.6" "- **Sandbox / Sandboxing** — CANONICAL. Phrase: *“runs in a sandbox”*\n- **Principle of Least Privilege** — CANONICAL. Phrase: *“least privilege is the first thing you have to build”*\n- **Reproducible Environments** — CANONICAL. Phrase: *“Environments are reproducible”*\n- **Immutable Infrastructure / Immutability** — CANONICAL. Phrase: *“immutable”*\n- **Privilege Escalation (prevention / no self-authorization)** — CANONICAL. Phrase: *“cannot grant them to itself”*\n- **Working Directory** — CANONICAL. Phrase: *“working directory”*\n- **Physical Access Compromise maxim** — CANONICAL. Phrase: *“physical access to secrets will get at them sooner or later”*\n- **Tick (as the agent’s execution cycle)** — AUTHOR’S. Phrase: *“for the duration of a tick”*\n- **Agent State = Sandbox + Working Directory** — AUTHOR’S model. Phrase: *“its whole state is the sandbox plus a working directory”*\n", "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5" "# Established Concepts in the Note\n\n1. **Sandboxing**\n   - Status: CANONICAL\n   - Phrase: \"Each agent runs in a sandbox prepared specifically for it\"\n\n2. **Principle of Least Privilege**\n   - Status: CANONICAL\n   - Phrase: \"so least privilege is the first thing you have to build\"\n\n3. **Physical Access Security Principle** (physical access defeats security controls)\n   - Status: CANONICAL\n   - Phrase: \"An agent with physical access to secrets will get at them sooner or later\"\n\n4. **Immutability**\n   - Status: CANONICAL\n   - Phrase: \"Environments are reproducible and immutable for the duration of a tick\"\n\n5. **Reproducibility** (reproducible environments/builds)\n   - Status: CANONICAL\n   - Phrase: \"Environments are reproducible and immutable for the duration of a tick\"\n\n6. **Privilege Escalation Prevention** (non-self-granting of privileges)\n   - Status: CANONICAL\n   - Phrase: \"an agent can request more rights but cannot grant them to itself\"\n"}, "extract-coinage-trap" {"opencode-go/deepseek-v4-flash" "NOTE’s concepts, classified:\n\n**Canonical/established** (terms the note effectively invokes):\n- **Fault isolation / failure containment** — “blast radius stays small”; blast radius is standard engineering vocabulary.\n- **Encapsulation** — the wrap-and-carry-only-what’s-needed structure.\n- **Least privilege / need-to-know scoping** — capsule “carries only the inputs it needs.”\n- **Reproducible (deterministic) construction** — rebuild from a manifest yields an identical result each run.\n- **Declarative manifest / infrastructure-as-code** — the manifest is the single source of truth for reconstruction.\n- **Modularity / decoupling** — confined failure surface per unit.\n\n**Author’s own coined term** (not established, do not treat as canonical):\n- **“context capsule”** — the author’s label; it bundles isolation, scoping, and reproducibility into one image. Not a recognized term in the field.\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-pro" "## Established concepts invoked\n\n| Concept in NOTE | Status |\n|---|---|\n| Blast radius (limiting failure scope) | Canonical-established — standard reliability/security term |\n| Isolation / sandboxing (wrap a task so it can't affect others) | Canonical-established |\n| Principle of least privilege (carry *only* the inputs needed) | Canonical-established |\n| Failure containment / fault isolation (one bug can't cascade) | Canonical-established |\n| Dependency injection (task receives its inputs rather than reaching for them) | Canonical-established |\n| Manifest (declarative spec for how to reconstruct something) | Canonical-established — e.g., package manifest, Dockerfile, lockfile |\n| Reproducibility (identical rebuild from the manifest) | Canonical-established |\n| Determinism (same specification → same output) | Canonical-established |\n| Idempotency (rebuild yields same result each run) | Canonical-established |\n\n## Author-coined term\n\n| Term | Status |\n|---|---|\n| \"Context capsule\" | Author's own coined term — self-declared (\"my term\"); not an established industry name |\n\nCanonical names for the *thing* the author calls a context capsule: isolated execution context, hermetic sandbox, or a deterministic build unit — but the label \"context capsule\" itself is the author's coinage, not established vocabulary.\n", "opencode-go/glm-5.2" "Analysis of the NOTE as given in the message.\n\n## Coined by the author\n\n- **\"Context capsule\"** — the author's own term, explicitly self-labeled (\"my term\"). Not established anywhere else. It's a wrapper concept implementing established ideas below.\n\n## Canonical-established concepts invoked\n\n| Concept in NOTE | Established name |\n|---|---|\n| \"a single bug can't cascade\" | Fault isolation; cascading failure containment |\n| \"the blast radius stays small\" | \"Blast radius\" — established industry idiom (informal, not a formal CS term) for impact scope of a fault or change |\n| \"carries only the inputs it needs\" | Minimal dependency / encapsulation; adjacent to principle of least privilege |\n| \"rebuild the capsule from a manifest\" | Manifest-driven build (Docker images, package managers, build systems) |\n| \"identical each time\" | Deterministic / reproducible builds; hermeticity |\n\nSummary: one coined term (\"context capsule\"). Everything else maps to well-established concepts — fault isolation, blast radius (informal idiom), manifests, deterministic/hermetic builds. The NOTE's novelty is packaging, not the underlying ideas.\n", "opencode-go/kimi-k2.6" "Canonical-established:\n- **Task isolation / encapsulation** — confining execution to a bounded environment.\n- **Minimal input set / dependency closure** — supplying only required inputs (cf. hermetic builds, sealed environments).\n- **Fault containment / failure propagation control** — preventing a single defect from triggering downstream failures.\n- **Blast radius** — established reliability-engineering metaphor for scope of impact.\n- **Manifest-driven / declarative reconstruction** — building state from a declared specification rather than mutable prior state.\n- **Determinism / reproducibility / idempotency** — identical output on every run from the same inputs.\n\nAuthor's coined term:\n- **Context capsule** — the author explicitly flags this as \"(my term)\" for the isolation unit described above.\n", "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5" "# Concepts in the Note\n\n## Canonical/Well-Established\n\n- **Task isolation** – containing work units to prevent interference\n- **Dependency injection** / **explicit inputs** – providing only required dependencies\n- **Fault isolation** – preventing errors from propagating beyond their origin\n- **Blast radius** – metaphor from explosives/military, widely adopted in reliability engineering to describe failure impact scope\n- **Manifest-driven builds** – declarative specifications of dependencies/configuration\n- **Reproducibility** / **determinism** – rebuilding identical artifacts from same inputs\n- **Idempotence** – producing the same result on repeated execution\n\n## Author's Coined Term\n\n- **\"context capsule\"** – explicitly marked as the author's term for the encapsulation mechanism they describe\n\n---\n\nThe author is applying established isolation and reproducibility patterns but has labeled their implementation \"context capsule.\"\n"}, "synth-idempotency" {"opencode-go/deepseek-v4-flash" "# Idempotency (computing/APIs)\n\n**Definition:** An operation is idempotent if performing it multiple times has the same effect as performing it once [1].\n\n**HTTP methods:** GET, HEAD, PUT, and DELETE are idempotent; POST is not [2].\n\n**Consequence:** Idempotent methods can be retried safely after a failure [2].\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-pro" "Idempotency (computing/APIs)\n\nAn operation is idempotent if performing it multiple times has the same effect as performing it once (Source 1). In HTTP, the methods `GET`, `HEAD`, `PUT`, and `DELETE` are idempotent; `POST` is not. Idempotent methods can be retried safely after a failure (Source 2).\n", "opencode-go/glm-5.2" "# Idempotency (computing/APIs)\n\n**Idempotency** is a property of an operation whereby performing it multiple times has the same effect as performing it once [1].\n\nIn the context of HTTP, the methods GET, HEAD, PUT and DELETE are idempotent, while POST is not [2]. A practical consequence is that idempotent methods can be retried safely after a failure [2].\n\n## References\n\n1. Wikipedia — \"An operation is idempotent if performing it multiple times has the same effect as performing it once.\"\n2. MDN, HTTP — \"GET, HEAD, PUT and DELETE are idempotent; POST is not. Idempotent methods can be retried safely after a failure.\"\n", "opencode-go/kimi-k2.6" "**Idempotency (computing/APIs)**\n\nAn operation is idempotent if performing it multiple times has the same effect as performing it once.[1] In HTTP, the methods GET, HEAD, PUT, and DELETE are idempotent; POST is not. Idempotent methods can be retried safely after a failure.[2]\n\n**Sources**\n\n[1] Wikipedia: \"An operation is idempotent if performing it multiple times has the same effect as performing it once.\"\n\n[2] MDN, HTTP: \"GET, HEAD, PUT and DELETE are idempotent; POST is not. Idempotent methods can be retried safely after a failure.\"\n", "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5" "# Idempotency (computing/APIs)\n\nAn operation is **idempotent** if performing it multiple times produces the same effect as performing it once.[¹] In HTTP, the methods GET, HEAD, PUT, and DELETE are idempotent, while POST is not.[²] This property allows idempotent methods to be safely retried after a failure without unintended side effects.[²]\n\n---\n**Sources:**  \n[1] Wikipedia  \n[2] MDN Web Docs (HTTP)\n"}, "synth-cap-bad-source" {"opencode-go/deepseek-v4-flash" "# CAP theorem\n\n**Definition.** CAP (Consistency, Availability, Partition tolerance), also called Brewer's theorem, states the behavior a distributed system can guarantee when its network partitions.\n\nThe popular form — *\"a system can only ever have two of C, A, and P, so you just pick any two\"* — is a simplification and, read literally, inaccurate.\n\n## Accurate statement\n- If the network **partitions** (nodes lose communication), a distributed data store cannot simultaneously guarantee both:\n  - **Consistency (C):** every read returns the most recent write.\n  - **Availability (A):** every request receives a response.\n  It must choose one, and sacrifice the other, while the partition lasts.\n- **Partition tolerance (P) is not an option.** A partition is an event that can happen; no design can \"opt out.\" The theorem addresses what a system may do *when* a partition occurs, not a property it selects.\n\n## Why \"pick any two\" is misleading\n- P is not a third selectable guarantee; it is the condition under which the C/A tradeoff applies. Systems are therefore usually described as **CP** or **AP *during* a partition**, not as having chosen two of three by design.\n- When no partition exists, the system can provide **both** C and A. The claim that a system always has exactly two of three is false outside partition conditions.\n- The aphorism also ignores that tradeoffs persist beyond partitions: even with no partition, enforcing consistency can cost latency (matters inside a consistency-vs-latency axis, cf. PACELC — nuance the source omits).\n\n## Notes\n- Origin: Eric Brewer's conjecture (2000); formal proof by Seth Gilbert and Nancy Lynch, *\"Brewer's Conjecture and the Feasibility of Consistent, Available, Partition-Tolerant Web Services\"* (2002).\n- Correct one-line summary: *during a network partition, a distributed system can guarantee either consistency or availability, but not both; partitions are an event, not a design choice.*\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-pro" "# CAP theorem\n\n| Field | Distributed systems |\n|---|---|\n| Also known as | Brewer’s theorem |\n| Formulated | Eric Brewer, 2000 |\n| Formalized | Seth Gilbert and Nancy Lynch, 2002 |\n\n## Definitions\n- **Consistency (C):** every read sees the result of the most recent write, or receives an error — not stale data.\n- **Availability (A):** every request to a non-failing node eventually receives a response, even if that response is not the latest data.\n- **Partition tolerance (P):** the system continues to operate despite arbitrary message loss or network partitions between nodes.\n\n## Statement\nA distributed data store cannot simultaneously guarantee consistency, availability, and partition tolerance. During a network partition, it must choose between **C** and **A** — either return an error or refuse some requests (safe, keeps C), or keep serving possibly stale data (loses C).\n\n## Accuracy note on the source\nThe source states: *“CAP says a distributed system can only ever have two of Consistency, Availability, and Partition tolerance, so you just pick any two.”*\n\nThis is a common, inaccurate oversimplification. Partition tolerance is not an optional property that can be deselected: in a real network, partitions can occur. The real choice under a partition is **C or A**; when there is no partition, a system can provide both C and A. Therefore “pick any two” misrepresents the theorem: it is not true that one simply chooses any pair of the three.\n", "opencode-go/glm-5.2" "**CAP theorem**\n\n**Field:** Distributed systems / Distributed computing\n\n**Statement:** In a distributed system that replicates shared data, when a network partition occurs the system cannot simultaneously guarantee consistency (linearizable reads/writes) and availability (every request at a non-failed node eventually responds). Under partition, it must sacrifice one: refuse or delay requests (preserving consistency) or serve possibly-stale data (preserving availability).\n\n**Terms:**\n- **C (Consistency):** All nodes see the same data at the same time; operationally, linearizability (in Gilbert & Lynch's formalization).\n- **A (Availability):** Every request received by a non-failing node receives a (non-error) response, without guarantee it reflects the newest write.\n- **P (Partition tolerance):** The system continues to operate despite the network dropping or arbitrarily delaying messages between nodes.\n\n**History:** Proposed by Eric Brewer in a 2000 PODC keynote; formalized as a conjecture by Seth Gilbert and Nancy Lynch (MIT) in 2002 and proven in their 2012 SIGACT News paper. Brewer later clarified (2012, *IEEE Computer*) that \"two of three\" is misleading and the real choice is C-vs-A *only during a partition*.\n\n**Common misconception (present in the cited source):** \"Pick any two of C, A, P\" is an oversimplification. Partition tolerance is not a feature you can decline to choose — real networks experience partitions, so P is effectively mandatory. Systems that \"choose CA\" are only consistent-and-available while no partition occurs; when one does, they must still pick C or A. In practice the tradeoff is also per-operation and tunable, not a global system property (e.g., PACELC: even without partitions, one trades latency vs. consistency).\n\n**Practical implication:** Guides datastore design — e.g., traditional RDBMS lean toward consistency; DNS and many NoSQL stores (Dynamo-style) lean toward availability under partition.\n\n---\n\n**Note on the source:** The supplied blog post states the \"pick any two\" version. That framing is a widely repeated misstatement — per your accuracy-first requirement, the card above corrects it rather than reproducing it: P cannot be traded away, and the C/A choice only binds during a partition.\n", "opencode-go/kimi-k2.6" "**CAP theorem** (also known as **Brewer’s theorem**)\n\n**Statement**  \nIn a distributed data store, it is impossible to simultaneously guarantee consistency, availability, and partition tolerance in the presence of a network partition.\n\n**Common simplification**  \nThe theorem is often summarized in informal writing as: *“A distributed system can only ever have two of Consistency, Availability, and Partition tolerance, so you just pick any two.”* This is an oversimplification. The formal proof by Gilbert and Lynch (2002) establishes that the trade-off applies specifically *during* a network partition: the system must then choose between consistency (C) and availability (A). Because partition tolerance is a practical prerequisite for any networked distributed system, the design decision is not an unconstrained “pick any two,” but rather how to balance consistency versus availability when a partition occurs.\n\n**Properties**\n- **Consistency** – Every read receives the most recent write or an error.\n- **Availability** – Every request receives a non-error response, without the guarantee that it contains the most recent write.\n- **Partition tolerance** – The system continues to operate despite network failures that prevent communication between nodes.\n\n**History**\n- **2000** – Conjectured by Eric Brewer.\n- **2002** – Formalized by Seth Gilbert and Nancy Lynch.\n", "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5" "# CAP Theorem\n\nThe **CAP theorem** states that in the presence of a **network partition**, a distributed system must choose between:\n\n- **Consistency** (C): All nodes see the same data at the same time\n- **Availability** (A): Every request receives a response (without guarantee of most recent data)\n\n**Key Point**: Partition tolerance (P) is not optional in distributed systems—network partitions will occur. The theorem describes the tradeoff between C and A *when partitions happen*.\n\n## Common Misconception\n\nThe phrase \"pick any two of CAP\" (as stated in the source) is a widespread misunderstanding. You cannot choose to avoid partitions in real-world distributed systems; partitions are a given constraint, not a design choice.\n\n**Accurate formulation**: During a partition, choose CP (sacrifice availability) or AP (sacrifice consistency).\n\n## Formulated By\nEric Brewer (2000), formally proven by Gilbert and Lynch (2002)\n\n---\n\n**Note**: The provided source presents the inaccurate \"pick any two\" interpretation, which has been corrected in academic literature and by Brewer himself.\n"}, "connect-correct" {"opencode-go/deepseek-v4-flash" "Confirmed — the claim is a textbook application of the concept, not a misinterpretation.\n\nConnection: payment capture has a side effect (moving money). A timeout leaves the client unsure whether the request succeeded, so retrying risks executing the side effect twice. Making the operation idempotent guarantees the second execution produces the same effect as the first — e.g., keying requests with an idempotency key so the server deduplicates retries and returns the original result (or a no-op) instead of charging again. Thus repetition yields the same observable effect as a single attempt.\n\nNo correction needed.\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-pro" "The claim correctly applies the concept.\n\nA timeout retry repeats the same payment operation. If the operation is idempotent, the retry yields the same effect as the first attempt — one charge, not two. If it is not idempotent, the repeated operation can produce a duplicate charge.\n\nNo misinterpretation.\n", "opencode-go/glm-5.2" "**Confirmed — correct application.**\n\nThe claim maps directly onto the definition: a payment charge is not naturally idempotent (executing it twice charges twice, not once), so a client retry after an ambiguous timeout — where the server may or may not have completed the charge — risks a duplicate effect. Idempotency repairs exactly this: if the operation yields the same effect when repeated as when performed once (e.g., keyed by an idempotency token so a retried request returns the original result instead of initiating a second charge), the retry becomes safe.\n\nOne refinement: the claim's phrase \"unless the operation is idempotent\" is the right condition, but the fix isn't the payment itself — it's making the *request handling* idempotent (same request → same recorded outcome, no second charge). That's the standard correction to a correct claim: no misinterpretation, just precision about where idempotency is enforced.\n", "opencode-go/kimi-k2.6" "**Confirm.** The claim correctly applies idempotency.\n\n**Connection:** Idempotency is the safeguard against the double-charge risk. A non-idempotent payment retry creates a second, distinct charge; an idempotent retry leaves the customer’s account in the same state as if the request had succeeded once—settling the charge exactly one time regardless of how many retries occurred.\n", "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5" "**Connection**: Correct application. A non-idempotent payment creates a new charge per request → retry doubles the effect. An idempotent payment uses a unique request ID to ensure multiple identical requests produce one charge → retry preserves the single-charge effect. Claim accurately identifies idempotency as the defense against duplicate charges on retry.\n"}, "connect-misinterpretation" {"opencode-go/deepseek-v4-flash" "**Connection: “Chose CA, dropped P” misstates CAP**\n\n**Flag:** Yes — misinterpreted. CAP never offers “drop partition tolerance.” P isn’t a feature you can decline; it’s the assumption that partitions occur, which any system spanning a real network inherits.\n\n**Correction**\n\n- The theorem governs *partitioned* operation: when nodes can’t communicate, the system must either (C) refuse some operations to stay consistent, or (A) keep answering, possibly with stale data. Both together are impossible *only while the partition lasts*.\n- “P” is therefore not a third menu item but the scenario itself. On a real network the only design question is which of C or A to sacrifice during a partition — so honest labels are **CP** or **AP**.\n- **CA is not a real distributed configuration.** It describes a single node (no network, no partitions), or an assumed-perfect network. A multi-node deployment claiming “CA, no P” has no defined behavior when the partition actually arrives — it just breaks one of the two promises silently.\n- The folklore “pick any 2 of 3” is wrong for the same reason: outside partitions, C and A are fully compatible with P-enabled systems. The tension is conditional, not a permanent budget.\n\n**Accurate restatement of the note:** “We replicate (so partitions are possible) and we prioritize consistency; during a partition, availability suffers.” That system is *CP*, not CA. If the intent was to keep serving during partitions, it’s *AP*. Either way, partition tolerance was never dropped — it was assumed.\n", "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-pro" "The note misinterprets CAP. Partition tolerance isn't an option to drop—partitions happen in real distributed systems, so the only meaningful choice during a partition is C versus A.\n\nCorrect phrasing: **\"We chose CP: under a partition, favor consistency over availability.\"** \"CA while dropping P\" isn't a valid CAP position.\n", "opencode-go/glm-5.2" "The claim misinterprets CAP. \"Partition tolerance\" isn't a property you can decline — in any real distributed system, networks *will* partition (cables fail, messages drop, nodes pause). CAP says: **when** a partition occurs, the system must sacrifice either consistency or availability. P is the precondition, not a menu option.\n\nSo \"chose CA\" is only meaningful as a description of behavior *absent* a partition — e.g., a single-node database is trivially CA. A distributed system labeled \"CA\" really means it's CP (rejects requests during a partition to preserve consistency) or AP (serves possibly-stale data to preserve availability) once a partition actually hits. Correct framing: the system chose C-over-A or A-over-C *under partition*; it did not and cannot drop P.\n", "opencode-go/kimi-k2.6" "Claim misinterprets CAP.\n\nThe theorem states: **given** a network partition, a distributed system must choose between consistency (C) and availability (A). Partition tolerance (P) is not a design choice—it is a physical reality of networked systems. Networks drop messages, nodes fail, and delays occur; a distributed system that does not tolerate partitions is simply not operational during failures.\n\nTherefore, “CA” (choosing consistency and availability while dropping partition tolerance) is only valid for non-distributed or single-node systems where partitions are impossible by design. In real distributed systems, the meaningful trade-off is **CP vs. AP**.\n", "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5" "**Misconception flagged.**\n\nThe claim reverses CAP: partition tolerance isn't a choice—partitions *will* occur in distributed systems. The theorem states that *during a partition*, you must choose between consistency (C) and availability (A). \"CA\" systems don't exist at scale because refusing to handle partitions means refusing distribution itself.\n\nThe real choice: CP (reject requests to preserve consistency) or AP (serve potentially stale data to stay available). Networks partition; the question is how you respond.\n"}}, :usage {"opencode-go/deepseek-v4-flash" {:provider "opencode-go", :delta 0}, "opencode-go/deepseek-v4-pro" {:provider "opencode-go", :delta 0}, "opencode-go/glm-5.2" {:provider "opencode-go", :delta 0}, "opencode-go/kimi-k2.6" {:provider "opencode-go", :delta 3}, "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5" {:provider "anthropic", :delta -1}}}